wolfenstein the old blood reunites BJ  blaskowitz with the setting that made  him famous castle wolfenstein itself hi  there this is Chris waters standing in  for reviewer Jeremy signor the first  part of this standalone DLC involves  infiltrating the castle in order to find  the location general deaths heads evil  Nazi base BJ is very much in his element  in the world war 2 setting compared to  the alternative history 1960s of the new  order and though there's still advanced  technology it's more in line with what  you'd expect from pulp Nazi super  science for the era firing guns feels  both effortless and flexible and most  weapons can be dual wielded the old  blood absent flows as it constantly  changes up each encounters focus  creating an excellent sense of pacing  action-packed shooting sequences in open  cover Laden levels alternate with more  freeform and surprisingly excellent  stealth sections that don't object to  you taking a more direct approach  there are even portions where you're  encouraged to run rather than fight  there's also some seriously great enemy  AI they'll flank you and take cover  keeping you on your toes yet they aren't  so smart that you can't outmaneuver them  pretty much everything about the way the  old blood plays exemplifies first-person  shooter design in 2015 and is just plain  fun to bj's local contact kessler is a  stark contrast to the burly protagonist  his weapon of choice being leaflets  rather than guns  he's also acutely empathetic of human  life in one memorable exchange he  implores BJ to avoid killing as many  people as he can which BJ then willfully  misinterprets to me that he should kill  as many Nazis as we can we also get a  glimpse of some character among the  enemy combatants albeit only through  memos and a scene where you walk through  a bar crowded with Nazi soldiers even  Helga von shop's the antagonist of the  game is granted some character  development in her lifelong fascination  with adventure and her potentially royal  lineage she also likes wine like a lot  even so BJ still sees his enemies as the  same flat characters he sees in his  Wolfenstein 3d fever dreams good for  only one thing killing the game's  mechanics almost never encourage you to  leave any Nazis alive the old blood is a  blunt muscular bare-chested romp through  levels full of deadly moving targets  even during it's subtle moments and  there's nothing wrong with that but it  fumbles when it attempts to introduce  gray areas into the game whether it's  Kessler's empathy for his oppressors or  the supplementary notes you find  scattered around the old blood seems to  have something to say about the  evolution of games but it never quite  arrives at a solid point  the sledgehammer that is BJ's philosophy  always wins out stranding the gray areas  in a game that clearly won't give them  the time of day the second half of the  old blood changes up enemy types by  replacing Nazis the default enemy type  of the old ERA with zombies it's a nice  change of pace from the terse shooting  areas that came before but they kind of  feel like a downgrade turning a  mechanically smart action game into a  brain-dead one don't get me wrong mowing  down zombies with a sawed-off shotgun  never really gets old per se but the  waves and waves of corpses pouring at  you can't help but feel disappointing  after you've experienced the smart  design of our  one still it's clear that machinegames  understands the nuance that can be  evoked in the medium and the old blood  certainly displays intelligent action  FPS design that's fun and effortless to  play just don't expect the deep  examination the game hints at to go  anywhere hear the gloriously dumb yet  smartly designed reins progress can wait  long live the old blood
 

